1
00:00:00,128 --> 00:00:02,808
So far we learned about those connectors

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,439
like Gmail and Calendar, which allow

3
00:00:06,113 --> 00:00:06,402
our

4
00:00:07,333 --> 00:00:10,350
AI to connect with different third-party

5
00:00:10,526 --> 00:00:10,767
apps.

6
00:00:11,345 --> 00:00:14,361
But what is the core idea behind those

7
00:00:14,474 --> 00:00:15,003
connectors?

8
00:00:15,581 --> 00:00:19,978
How can an AI model and an app communicate

9
00:00:20,074 --> 00:00:20,732
with each other?

10
00:00:21,454 --> 00:00:24,359
Well, both the model and the app need a

11
00:00:24,567 --> 00:00:27,151
common language to talk to each other.

12
00:00:27,713 --> 00:00:28,484
And the language

13
00:00:29,062 --> 00:00:30,910
that allows them to communicate with each

14
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:33,207
other is called MCP,

15
00:00:33,737 --> 00:00:35,697
Model Context Protocol.

16
00:00:36,452 --> 00:00:39,777
It's an open standard built by Anthropic

17
00:00:40,146 --> 00:00:43,423
that lets any app expose its capabilities to

18
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:45,223
Claude in a uniform way.

19
00:00:45,929 --> 00:00:47,166
And every connector

20
00:00:47,696 --> 00:00:49,929
we have learned about so far, so all those

21
00:00:50,026 --> 00:00:51,198
connectors in here

22
00:00:51,873 --> 00:00:54,749
are actually MCP servers.

23
00:00:55,648 --> 00:00:58,586
Anthropic has just packaged those as a

24
00:00:58,715 --> 00:01:01,155
one-click install with the authentication

25
00:01:01,203 --> 00:01:02,568
flow already wired up.

26
00:01:03,227 --> 00:01:06,245
So when you add a custom connector now, so

27
00:01:06,277 --> 00:01:06,839
in our

28
00:01:07,867 --> 00:01:10,051
plus button, add custom connector,

29
00:01:10,982 --> 00:01:13,358
you are doing the same thing without that

30
00:01:13,631 --> 00:01:14,097
packaging.

31
00:01:14,755 --> 00:01:18,352
You point Cowork at an MCP server directly

32
00:01:18,705 --> 00:01:20,182
by a URL.

33
00:01:20,792 --> 00:01:23,037
It's the same idea underneath, just a

34
00:01:23,117 --> 00:01:25,234
different level of convenience, so to say,

35
00:01:25,507 --> 00:01:28,377
because obviously Anthropic can't give you

36
00:01:28,794 --> 00:01:32,194
preconfigured connectors, preconfigured MCP

37
00:01:32,194 --> 00:01:35,850
servers for every app out there. Now, if we

38
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:38,800
look closer at what an MCP actually is,

39
00:01:39,394 --> 00:01:43,370
an MCP simply exposes a set of tools to

40
00:01:43,547 --> 00:01:44,012
Claude.

41
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:48,166
A tool is just a named action. In the Gmail

42
00:01:48,279 --> 00:01:51,041
case, this could be send email, search

43
00:01:51,105 --> 00:01:52,856
inbox, or create issue.

44
00:01:53,659 --> 00:01:57,176
The Gmail MCP server exposes one set of

45
00:01:57,208 --> 00:02:00,837
tools. Another MCP server exposes another

46
00:02:00,837 --> 00:02:03,632
set of tools, but Claude calls them all the

47
00:02:03,632 --> 00:02:06,715
same way. So adding such a server or

48
00:02:07,004 --> 00:02:10,377
enabling such a connector in Claude is what

49
00:02:10,377 --> 00:02:13,268
gives Claude tool access for that specific

50
00:02:13,412 --> 00:02:14,039
application.

51
00:02:14,777 --> 00:02:18,776
And an MCP server is just a small program

52
00:02:19,129 --> 00:02:21,939
that sits between Claude and the specific

53
00:02:22,084 --> 00:02:22,196
app.

54
00:02:22,838 --> 00:02:26,275
So it knows that app's API, its endpoints,

55
00:02:26,355 --> 00:02:29,069
its data formats, its authentication, and

56
00:02:29,101 --> 00:02:33,147
exposes those abilities as MCP tools.

57
00:02:33,918 --> 00:02:37,579
So each app needs its own MCP server custom

58
00:02:37,595 --> 00:02:38,061
built for it.

59
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:41,208
Anthropic built some MCPs. Google built some

60
00:02:41,208 --> 00:02:44,549
MCPs. Other teams build theirs, anyone can

61
00:02:44,581 --> 00:02:46,412
build one for their app.

62
00:02:47,054 --> 00:02:49,607
So when you tell Claude, send Markus an

63
00:02:49,864 --> 00:02:52,739
email about being late, 2 translations

64
00:02:52,835 --> 00:02:53,108
happen.

65
00:02:53,799 --> 00:02:57,123
First, Claude reads your sentence and turns

66
00:02:57,123 --> 00:02:58,986
it into a structured tool call.

67
00:02:59,532 --> 00:03:02,134
So use the send email tool with these

68
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:02,872
arguments.

69
00:03:03,531 --> 00:03:05,859
That's Claude's job though. The second

70
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:08,927
translation that happens is the MCP server

71
00:03:09,232 --> 00:03:11,220
taking that structured call

72
00:03:11,877 --> 00:03:15,197
and translating it into the actual Gmail API

73
00:03:15,325 --> 00:03:15,533
call.

74
00:03:16,111 --> 00:03:19,622
That's the MCP server's job. So Claude only

75
00:03:19,911 --> 00:03:22,332
speaks to the MCP server.

76
00:03:22,925 --> 00:03:25,956
All the app-specific complexity lives inside

77
00:03:26,068 --> 00:03:26,870
this server.

78
00:03:28,297 --> 00:03:30,926
And you might also wonder why Claude

79
00:03:31,039 --> 00:03:33,364
couldn't just write a Python script, for

80
00:03:33,364 --> 00:03:36,699
example, that calls Gmail's API directly.

81
00:03:37,517 --> 00:03:39,637
Technically, this is possible, but there's a

82
00:03:39,701 --> 00:03:40,134
problem.

83
00:03:40,809 --> 00:03:41,628
Every script

84
00:03:42,174 --> 00:03:45,048
needs the API keys built in, so you need

85
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:47,874
that authentication to interact with the

86
00:03:47,874 --> 00:03:48,580
application.

87
00:03:49,174 --> 00:03:52,209
And once you expose these keys to the large

88
00:03:52,225 --> 00:03:55,019
language model, you kind of lose control

89
00:03:55,115 --> 00:03:55,469
over them,

90
00:03:56,079 --> 00:03:58,246
and they could end up in a file, as chat

91
00:03:58,246 --> 00:04:00,896
output, as a log, so anywhere where you

92
00:04:00,896 --> 00:04:02,228
don't want to have your keys.

93
00:04:02,982 --> 00:04:05,450
This is also solved by MCPs.

94
00:04:06,027 --> 00:04:09,922
The credentials stay inside the MCP server,

95
00:04:10,210 --> 00:04:12,775
so Claude only knows the tool calls it's

96
00:04:12,839 --> 00:04:15,996
allowed to make. The keys never cross into

97
00:04:16,044 --> 00:04:16,974
Claude's view,

98
00:04:17,567 --> 00:04:19,730
and that's one of the real values of MCPs.

99
00:04:20,435 --> 00:04:23,256
It's not just a translator, as I talked

100
00:04:23,256 --> 00:04:26,349
about, it's also a security boundary.

101
00:04:27,022 --> 00:04:28,946
And that again brings us back to our

102
00:04:28,946 --> 00:04:31,734
connectors and the custom connectors we

103
00:04:31,734 --> 00:04:34,252
talked about. Because if you have a look at

104
00:04:34,252 --> 00:04:36,963
the official documentation, you'll see

105
00:04:36,963 --> 00:04:40,380
connectors and MCP listed as separate

106
00:04:40,428 --> 00:04:41,182
sections

107
00:04:41,759 --> 00:04:43,684
in the documentation and here in the user

108
00:04:43,781 --> 00:04:44,374
interface.

109
00:04:44,904 --> 00:04:47,615
But the underlying technology we just had a

110
00:04:47,615 --> 00:04:50,165
look at is the same. So don't get confused

111
00:04:50,197 --> 00:04:54,111
by that. Connectors are basically prebuilt

112
00:04:54,192 --> 00:04:54,849
MCPs,

113
00:04:55,411 --> 00:04:58,266
which make using those apps as convenient as

114
00:04:58,459 --> 00:05:00,002
possible for you. But

115
00:05:00,535 --> 00:05:04,284
using custom connectors is just using MCPs

116
00:05:04,752 --> 00:05:08,275
by adding the name and the server URL of

117
00:05:08,453 --> 00:05:09,277
that MCP.
